<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Truso</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th–10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Truso was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> trading settlement on the shore of Lake Drużno near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbląg</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, active from the 8th to the 10th century. The traveller Wulfstan, whose voyage from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a> is recorded in the Old English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orosius">Orosius</a>, described sailing there around 890. Excavations at Janów Pomorski since 1982 have identified the site and found evidence of crafts, coins and long-distance trade.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Truso was a port of trade set up by Scandinavians on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogat">Nogat</a> branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> delta, close to a bay that is now Lake Drużno, where the river emptied into the shallow, brackish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula-lagoon">Vistula Lagoon</a>. It lay on the Amber Road, which drew merchants from central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> who supplied the markets of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, and on routes leading from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birka">Birka</a> through Visby to the southern Baltic coast. Westward routes ran from Truso and its rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiskiauten">Wiskiauten</a> along the Baltic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Wulfstan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a> made his seven-day voyage to Truso at the behest of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>. His account names the lands he passed under sail, with Weonodland, the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a>, on his right as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> estuary and lands subject to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and to the Sweons on his left, and places Truso near Estmere, his name for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula-lagoon">Vistula Lagoon</a>. Historians still debate the purpose of the voyage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marija-gimbutas">Marija Gimbutas</a> called the name of the town the earliest known historically in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> area.</p><p>The goods most sought at Truso were amber, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and pagan slaves, and its smiths and amber workers made goods for trade. In the second half of the 10th century silting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogat">Nogat</a> began to cut the town off from the lagoon and the sea, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>, on the coast, rose in its place as the local trading centre. Mateusz Bogucki holds that a strong political power, probably of Piast origin, sent warriors to take control and destroyed the town.</p><p>Searches for the site began in the early 16th century. Finds of 1897 and excavations from the 1920s placed it at Janów Pomorski near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbląg</a>, and in the 1980s Marek Jagodziński cleared about 20 hectares, where a series of structures had burnt down around the year 1,000. Finds include scales, weights, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> horseshoe brooches, swords, coins and Scandinavian jewellery, and long houses of about 6 by 21 metres. Scandinavians lived in the central and port area, while Balts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> may have lived on the edges.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Truso” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its Prussian inhabitants lived near the old trading centre of <b>Truso</b>, a Viking Age emporium described by the traveller Wulfstan around 890, and they are known from Peter of Dusburg's chronicle.</blockquote><a href="/state/pogesanians">Pogesanians</a></li></ul></section>
